Oracle SRE Interview Experience – A Complete Journey 🚀

I recently completed all 5 rounds of interviews for the Site Reliability Engineer (SRE) role at Oracle, and it was one of the most enriching and technically deep interview experiences I’ve ever had.

Here’s a quick overview of how the entire process went 👇

Round 1 – Screening / Coding Round (1 hour)
The first round was conducted on HackerRank and included 3 coding questions:
1. Bash scripting problem
2. Jenkins pipeline task
3. Kubernetes Liveness & Readiness Probe implementation
It was a great mix of scripting, CI/CD, and Kubernetes fundamentals.

Round 2 – Technical Round (1 hr 20 mins)
This was an in-depth DevOps + SRE technical discussion.
They asked me to write a Python script on HackerRank, followed by questions on:  My project architecture
Core DevOps tools — Docker, Kubernetes, Terraform
SRE principles — with real-world scenario-based questions
This round tested both my technical knowledge and real-world problem-solving approach.

Round 3 – System Design + Technical Deep Dive (1 hour)
This was purely system design-focused, covering:
AWS architecture
Jenkins pipelines and optimization
Terraform modules and best practices
Kubernetes and Docker internals
SRE-based troubleshooting scenarios
It was a true deep-dive into scalability, reliability, and automation.

Round 4 – Managerial + Cultural Fit (1 hr 10 mins)
This round evaluated my team fit, culture alignment, and leadership mindset.
They also asked scenario-based technical questions where I had to use my hands-on experience to answer practical challenges — not just theory.

Round 5 – Hiring Manager Round (~1 hour)
The final round was a detailed discussion about:
My past projects and architecture
Tech stack expertise (SRE + DevOps)
Real-world experience in incident management, automation, and reliability improvements
Combination of technical + behavioral questions
This round was all about connecting my real-world impact to Oracle’s expectations.

Overall Experience
The entire process was smooth, transparent, and extremely well-structured.
Every interviewer was deeply technical yet approachable, and the discussions were focused on practical knowledge, not just theory.
